    "result_text": "Agreed with the finding, and fixed. Tests still green (`customerCode=NSW-16E6609E`, BUILD SUCCESS).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe postcode\u2192city\u2192`\"UNKNOWN\"` chain now lives in exactly one place:\n\n- **`PostcodeRules.resolveRegion(postcode, city)`** (new static helper) \u2014 composes the existing `regionOfPostcode` lookup with the `CityRegions.regionOf` fallback and the `\"UNKNOWN\"` default. This is the natural home: `PostcodeRules` already bridges both lookups (its `isValidForCity` consults `CityRegions`), and it sits \"alongside\" the two \"single source of truth\" tables as the finding suggested.\n- **`ClinicServiceImpl.generateCustomerCode`** now calls `PostcodeRules.resolveRegion(...)` directly; the duplicated private `resolveRegion` is gone, and the now-unused `CityRegions` import was removed.\n- **`OwnerMapper.toLocality`** keeps reading the REGION segment off the `customerCode`, but its fallback delegates to `PostcodeRules.resolveRegion(...)` instead of re-implementing the chain; its unused `CityRegions` import was removed too.\n\nThe lock-step promise in the Javadoc is now structurally guaranteed \u2014 both the identity's REGION and the locality fallback resolve through the same single method, so a change to the fallback order or default can't drift between them.\n\nAcceptance behaviour is unchanged: the resolution produces identical values, and the one acceptance test passes.",
